<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  mail42.entitlements — signed into the release binary (scripts/build-mail42.sh, scripts/release.sh). An ad-hoc development
  build carries none of these.

  The release is built with the Hardened Runtime (notarization requires it). Full Disk Access, which mail42 needs
  to read Apple Mail's files, is a user grant in System Settings, not an entitlement. Two entitlements:

  - Apple Events: with the Hardened Runtime, a process may only send Apple Events to another app when it carries
    it, and mail42 asks Mail (mark, move, archive, delete, junk, drafts). macOS still asks the user once whether
    mail42 may control Mail (Privacy & Security › Automation).
  - The app group 74RFQFPVQ9.com.work42.mail42 (WOR-889). `mail42 serve` listens in that group's container
    (~/Library/Group Containers/74RFQFPVQ9.com.work42.mail42), because Work42's mail42 widget runs in a sandboxed extension
    that can connect to a socket there and nowhere in a home folder. Carrying the group also keeps macOS from asking
    whether mail42 may "access data from other apps" when it opens its own group's folder. The group starts with the
    team id, so it needs no provisioning profile.

  No sandbox.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
    <array>
        <string>74RFQFPVQ9.com.work42.mail42</string>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
